      "To most city dwellers, sand appears virtually inexhaustible—blanketing vast deserts from the Sahara to the Arabian Peninsula and lining thousands of kilometers of tourist beaches. Construction engineers know a starkly different reality. Desert sand grains, tumbled against one another by wind for millennia, are too rounded and smooth to bind tightly inside structural concrete mortar. Builders of apartment towers, expressways, bridges, and coastal land-reclamation projects require angular, rough-edged aggregate scoured by moving water, turning riverbeds, floodplains, and lakeshores into the world's most heavily extracted solid mineral resource after water itself.\n\nAcross rapidly urbanizing river basins in South and Southeast Asia, East Africa, and Latin America, mechanized suction barges and nocturnal excavators now strip riverbed sand far faster than upstream mountain erosion can replenish it. Removing tens of millions of tons annually triggers cascading physical damage along entire river corridors. Channel beds incise downward by several meters, leaving irrigation intake canals high and dry, undermining highway bridge pilings, and dropping adjacent floodplain water tables during dry seasons. Farther downstream, starving river mouths of natural sediment causes densely populated agricultural deltas—from the Mekong to the Nile and Pearl—to sink and erode into rising seas while salt wedges push tens of kilometers inland into rice paddies. Unregulated aggregate extraction thus (23).\n\nCurbing destructive river mining without halting essential housing and infrastructure construction requires coordinated material substitution and basin-wide sediment accounting. Materials laboratories have demonstrated that crushing quarry rock into manufactured sand, recycling demolished urban concrete, and optimizing structural beam geometries can replace substantial shares of virgin river aggregate without compromising compressive strength. Simultaneously, hydrological agencies are deploying satellite radar and acoustic bathymetry to set enforceable \"sediment budgets\" paired with electronic transport permits that trace sand from licensed pits to batching plants. (24), governments can treat river sediment not as free gravel for the taking, but as vital ecological capital. Protecting deltas in the twenty-first century ultimately demands that planners (25).",
