      "Why Bilingual Courtroom Interpreting Cannot Be Treated as Mechanical Word Swapping",
      "Television legal dramas routinely depict courtroom interpreters as invisible human conduits—translating every spoken English or Spanish sentence instantaneously word for word without altering emphasis, hesitation, or tone. Sociolinguists who transcribe real criminal trials and asylum hearings across multilingual jurisdictions tell a far more sobering story. Languages differ not only in vocabulary, but in how grammar encodes politeness, evidential certainty, agency, and conversational hedging. Treating legal interpreting as mechanical lexical substitution risks distorting how judges and jurors evaluate a witness's credibility before a single piece of physical evidence is weighed.\n\nEmpirical courtroom studies by linguist Susan Berk-Seligson and subsequent legal scholars revealed how seemingly minor pragmatic shifts alter fact-finders' impressions. Witness testimony delivered in discourse styles rich in polite hedges (\"sort of,\" \"it seems to me\"), honorifics, or passive constructions—used in many languages to show respect toward authority—frequently sounds evasive or uncertain when rendered literally into brisk adversarial cross-examination, whereas stripping those discourse markers out makes a hesitant witness appear artificially dogmatic. Conversely, prosecuting attorneys deliberately deploy tag questions (\"You didn't lock the warehouse gate, did you?\") whose coercive syntax has no exact grammatical twin in many target languages, forcing an interpreter working at 150 words per minute in real time to choose on the fly how forcefully to frame the question. Untrained or fatigued interpreting can therefore (26).\n\nProtecting due process in multilingual courts requires institutional safeguards that match the cognitive difficulty of legal interpreting. Leading appellate jurisdictions mandate rigorous national certification exams, assign two-interpreter teams who alternate every thirty minutes during long trials to prevent working-memory exhaustion, and record both source-language testimony and interpreted audio so disputed nuances can be audited on appeal—rather than archiving only the target-language transcript as an unquestionable official record. Judges and trial lawyers also receive training to avoid compound multi-clause questions and cultural idioms designed to trip witnesses up. (27), courts preserve the evidentiary integrity of non-native testimony. Equal justice across languages depends on recognizing that faithful interpretation is (28).",
      "テレビの法廷ドラマは法廷通訳人を透明な人間導管のように描きがちだ。語調やためらい、強調を少しも変えず一語一語を瞬時に別言語へ置き換えているかのように見せるのである。しかし多言語法域で実際の刑事裁判や難民認定審問を文字起こしして分析する社会言語学者は、はるかに厳しい現実を報告している。言語は語彙だけでなく、丁寧さ・証拠的確実性・行為主体性・控えめな緩衝表現を文法がどう符号化するかの点でも異なる。法廷通訳を機械的な単語置換として扱えば、物的証拠が一つ検討される前に裁判官や陪審員による証人の信用性評価が歪められかねない。言語学者スーザン・バーク＝セリグソンらの実証研究は、一見ささいな語用論上のずれが事実認定者の心証をどう左右するかを明らかにした。権威への敬意を示すために多くの言語で用いられる丁寧な緩衝表現や敬語、受身表現を多用する話し方は、対決型の反対尋問の場へ直訳されると曖昧ではぐらかしているように響きやすく、逆にそれらを削ぎ落とせばためらいがちな証人が不自然に断定的に見えてしまう。また検察官が意図的に使う付加疑問文（「倉庫の門に鍵をかけなかった、そうですね？」）の誘導的構文は多くの言語に厳密な文法的対応物がないため、毎分150語で同時処理する通訳人は質問の圧力をどう訳出するか瞬時の判断を迫られる。したがって未熟または疲労した通訳は(26)のである。多言語法廷で適正手続を守るには認知的負荷に見合う制度的保障が不可欠であり、先進法域では厳格な国家資格試験、作業記憶の消耗を防ぐ長時間の公判での30分交代2名体制、さらに訳文の速記録だけを絶対視せず原発言と通訳音声の双方を録音保存して控訴審で検証可能にする運用を導入し、判事や弁護士にも複合質問を避ける研修を行っている。(27)、裁判所は非母語証言の証拠的完全性を守ることができる。言語の壁を越えた公正な裁判は、忠実な通訳とは(28)であると認識することにかかっている。",
