      "Why High-Altitude Tropical Glaciers Matter Far Beyond Tourist Postcards",
      "Most people associate glaciers with polar ice sheets or high-latitude fjords in Alaska, Norway, and Patagonia, yet ninety-nine percent of the world's tropical glaciers perch above 4,800 meters along the South American Andes, above all in Peru and Bolivia, with tiny remnants clinging to peaks in East Africa and New Guinea. Because equatorial mountain air receives intense year-round solar radiation without a long winter freeze, tropical icefields respond with extraordinary sensitivity to even half-degree rises in freezing-line elevation—losing more than half their surface area since the 1970s. For millions of downstream farmers, city residents, and hydropower operators along the arid Pacific slope of Peru, vanishing ice is not merely a scenic tragedy, but a looming hydrological tipping point.\n\nIn monsoonal and tropical Andean climates where eighty to ninety percent of rain and snow falls during a single wet half-year between November and April, high mountain glaciers act as natural seasonal buffer reservoirs. During wet months they accumulate snow; throughout the cloudless May-to-October dry season, steady glacial meltwater sustains rivers such as Peru's Santa and Rimac that irrigate export asparagus and avocado valleys, spin high-head hydroelectric turbines, and supply drinking water to Lima and Huaraz. Initially, rapid glacier retreat produces a deceptive temporary windfall called \"peak water,\" swelling dry-season river flows for a decade or two as centuries of stored ice liquidate. Once ice volume shrinks past a critical threshold, however, dry-season runoff drops precipitously—while newly exposed pyritic bedrock along freshly deglaciated headwaters oxidizes in air and rain, leaching sulfuric acid and dissolved heavy metals such as arsenic, lead, and cadmium directly into mountain streams. Retreating Andean glaciers therefore (23).\n\nAdapting highland watersheds to a post-glacial dry season demands blending modern civil engineering with pre-Columbian hydrological craft. Communities across Peru are rehabilitating centuries-old stone infiltration canals known as amunas, which divert muddy wet-season mountain torrents onto permeable fractured hillsides so water percolates slowly through bedrock aquifers and re-emerges months later at downslope springs. At the same time, utilities are constructing engineered alpine wetlands planted with metal-accumulating sedges to neutralize acid rock drainage while expanding off-river storage reservoirs below unstable glacial lakes prone to sudden outburst floods. (24), Andean basins can cushion dry-season water scarcity and water-quality shocks simultaneously. Surviving tropical deglaciation ultimately requires water planners to (25).",
      "氷河といえば極地の氷床やアラスカ・ノルウェー・パタゴニアの高緯度フィヨルドを連想する人が多いが、世界の熱帯氷河の99％は南米アンデス山脈――とりわけペルーとボリビア――の標高4,800メートル以上に位置し、東アフリカやニューギニアの高峰にわずかな残片が残るのみである。赤道直下の高山の空気は長い冬の凍結期がないまま一年中強い日射を受けるため、熱帯氷河は凍結高度がわずか0.5度分上昇しただけでも極めて敏感に反応し、1970年代以降すでに面積の半分以上を失った。ペルー太平洋側の乾燥斜面に暮らす何百万人もの農民や都市住民、水力発電事業者にとって、氷の消失は単なる景観の喪失ではなく差し迫った水文学的転換点である。降水量の8〜9割が11〜4月の雨季に集中する熱帯アンデスでは、高山氷河が天然の季節調整ダムとして機能する。雨季に雪を蓄え、雲一つない5〜10月の乾季を通じて安定した融氷水をサンタ川やリマク川へ供給し、輸出向けアスパラガスやアボカドの灌漑、高落差水力発電、リマやワラスの飲料水を支えてきた。氷河後退の初期には何世紀も蓄えられた氷が一気に溶け出すことで乾季流量が10〜20年間一時的に増える「ピーク・ウォーター」という見せかけの恩恵が生じる。しかし氷体積が臨界点を割り込むと乾季の流出量は急減し、しかも氷が消えて新たに露出した黄鉄鉱を含む岩盤が空気と雨で酸化され、硫酸やヒ素・鉛・カドミウムなどの重金属が渓流へ直接溶出し始める。したがって後退するアンデス氷河は(23)のである。氷河なき乾季に高地流域を適応させるには近代土木工学と先コロンブス期の水利技術の融合が要る。ペルー各地の共同体は雨季の濁流を浸透性の高い山腹へ導いて岩盤帯水層にゆっくり涵養し数カ月後の乾季に下方の湧水として湧出させる古代の石積み浸透水路「アムナ」を修復しており、水道・電力事業者は金属を吸着するスゲ類を植えた人工高地湿地で酸性岩盤排水を中和しつつ氷河湖決壊洪水の危険がある湖の下流で調整池を整備している。(24)、アンデス流域は乾季の水不足と水質悪化の双方を和らげることができる。熱帯氷河消失の時代を生き抜くには水資源計画者が(25)必要がある。",
